package com.example.smartplantcare.ML

import com.example.smartplantcare.data.DiseaseResult

object DiseaseRepository {

    private val database: Map<String, DiseaseResult> = mapOf(

        // ==========================================
        // CHILI DISEASES
        // ==========================================
        "Chili_Bacterial_Spot" to DiseaseResult(
            diseaseName = "Bacterial Spot (Chili)",
            description = "Small, dark, water-soaked spots appear first on older lower leaves, rapidly turning brown to black with yellow halos surrounding them. Heavily infected leaves drop early, exposing the plant to stress and sunscald. Fruit develops raised, rough, scab-like lesions that reduce marketability even when the plant survives.",
            causes = "Caused by Xanthomonas euvesicatoria and related Xanthomonas species. Bacteria enter through natural leaf openings (stomata) or wounds and spread explosively through splashing rain, wind-driven water, contaminated tools, and bare hands. Disease develops fastest at 24–30°C with high rainfall or overhead irrigation. Infected seed and diseased transplants are the most common entry points into a new planting.",
            treatment = "Prune and discard spotted leaves in sealed trash using shears cleaned with rubbing alcohol between cuts. Avoid handling wet foliage and stop overhead watering immediately. For persistent spread, consult a local garden center or agricultural extension advisor about a labeled garden copper bactericide and follow product instructions carefully.",
            preventionTips = "Use certified disease-free seed and healthy transplants. Switch from overhead to soil-level or drip irrigation, space plants generously for airflow, clear fallen leaf debris, sanitize tools between plants, and rotate peppers away from the same soil in future seasons.",
            careTips = "Prune and remove spotted leaves promptly using clean shears disinfected with rubbing alcohol between cuts, and place removed foliage in a sealed trash bag rather than composting. Avoid overhead watering and water only at the soil base to keep leaves dry, since splashing water spreads bacterial spot rapidly. Do not handle plants while the foliage is wet, and clear fallen leaf debris from around the plant base. If symptoms continue spreading, consult a local garden center or agricultural extension office about an appropriate garden copper-based bactericide and follow the product label carefully. For future plantings, use certified disease-free seeds or healthy transplants, space plants for airflow, and rotate crops away from peppers and tomatoes."
        ),

        "Chili_Cercospora_Leaf_Spot" to DiseaseResult(
            diseaseName = "Cercospora Leaf Spot (Chili)",
            description = "Circular spots with a distinctive light gray-to-tan papery center and dark brown border — the classic 'frog-eye' pattern — form first on older lower leaves. As spots multiply, the central tissue may drop out, leaving shot-hole openings. Leaves yellow and drop prematurely, and in severe outbreaks the entire lower canopy can defoliate, exposing fruit to sunscald and dramatically cutting photosynthesis.",
            causes = "Caused by the fungus Cercospora capsici. Wind and splashing water disperse spores, and infection progresses fastest when leaf surfaces remain wet for 12 or more hours at temperatures above 25°C. Dense plantings, poor airflow, and overhead irrigation extend leaf wetness periods and worsen outbreaks. Spores also persist on infected debris in the soil between seasons.",
            treatment = "Remove leaves showing frog-eye spots into sealed trash rather than composting, sanitize shears after cutting, and thin overcrowded foliage to improve airflow. If spotting keeps spreading, consult a local garden center or agricultural extension advisor about an appropriate labeled garden fungicide for peppers.",
            preventionTips = "Use disease-free seed and inspect transplants before planting. Water strictly at soil level instead of overhead, clear fallen plant debris into household trash after harvest, and practice multi-season crop rotation away from peppers and related crops.",
            careTips = "Remove and discard leaves showing frog-eye spots in sealed household trash rather than composting, and sanitize pruning shears between cuts. Water strictly at soil level to keep foliage dry, and lightly thin overcrowded interior foliage to improve airflow and speed drying. Clear fallen debris around the base of the plant. For persistent fungal spotting, consider a labeled garden fungicide suitable for peppers after checking with a local garden center or agricultural extension advisor, and follow all label directions. Prevent recurrence by using disease-free seed, ensuring adequate plant spacing, and rotating chili out of the same bed in future seasons."
        ),

        "Chili_Curl_Virus" to DiseaseResult(
            diseaseName = "Leaf Curl Virus (Chili)",
            description = "Leaves curl sharply upward and inward, become thickened, leathery, and brittle, with veins swollen and internodes shortened — giving the plant a stunted, bunched appearance. New growth is severely reduced. Flowering drops sharply and fruit set fails almost entirely in plants infected early, often leading to complete yield loss. Older infected leaves develop a rough texture and older leaves become brittle.",
            causes = "Caused by begomoviruses (family Geminiviridae), most commonly Chili Leaf Curl Virus (ChiLCV), transmitted exclusively by the whitefly Bemisia tabaci while feeding on plant sap. The virus cannot spread through soil, water, or casual contact — whiteflies are the sole vector. The virus also persists in alternative hosts including tobacco, tomato, and surrounding weeds, making weed control critical. Warm, dry conditions favor rapid whitefly population buildup.",
            treatment = "No product cures a plant virus once infected. Bag and remove severely curled, stunted plants in household trash—never compost them—to protect neighboring crops, and manage whiteflies on healthy surrounding plants with insecticidal soap or neem oil applied to leaf undersides per label directions.",
            preventionTips = "Install yellow sticky traps near canopy height to monitor whiteflies, clear surrounding weeds that harbor pests, consider reflective mulch before transplanting, and choose resistant or tolerant chili varieties when available.",
            careTips = "There is no cure for viral infections once a chili plant is affected. If the plant is severely curled and stunted, carefully bag and remove it in the household trash rather than composting it so whiteflies cannot spread the virus to nearby healthy plants. Inspect the undersides of surrounding healthy leaves for whiteflies, clear nearby weeds that harbor pests, and consider yellow sticky traps or reflective mulch to help deter whiteflies. For active whitefly populations on healthy neighboring plants, apply insecticidal soap or horticultural neem oil thoroughly to leaf undersides following product label instructions. In future plantings, choose curl-virus-tolerant chili varieties when available."
        ),

        "Chili_Healthy_Leaf" to DiseaseResult(
            diseaseName = "Healthy Chili Plant",
            description = "Leaves are uniformly deep green, firm, and free of spots, curling, discoloration, or physical damage. Growth is steady and upright with no stunting. Flowering and fruit set proceed normally with no abnormal drop.",
            causes = "The result of consistently good agricultural practice: adequate plant spacing for airflow, balanced NPK fertilization, correct watering depth and timing, 6+ hours of direct sun daily, and regular scouting that catches pests or disease at the earliest possible stage before they establish.",
            treatment = "No active disease is present. Maintain steady soil-level watering, full sunlight, balanced garden feeding per package instructions, and regular leaf inspections.",
            preventionTips = "Water at the soil base to keep leaves dry, ensure soil drains well without staying waterlogged, keep the bed weed-free, prune yellowing lower leaves for airflow, and check leaf undersides regularly for pests or early spots.",
            careTips = "Your chili plant appears healthy and shows no signs of active disease. Maintain steady, deep watering at soil level without wetting the foliage, and ensure the soil drains well rather than staying waterlogged. Provide at least six hours of direct sunlight daily and feed periodically with a balanced garden fertilizer following package directions. Keep the area around the stem clean and weed-free, prune yellowing lower leaves to improve airflow, and inspect leaf undersides regularly for early signs of whiteflies, aphids, or spotting."
        ),

        "Chili_Nutrition_Deficiency" to DiseaseResult(
            diseaseName = "Nutrient Deficiency (Chili)",
            description = "Symptoms vary clearly by the nutrient involved: uniform pale yellowing from the lower leaves upward indicates nitrogen deficiency; purple or reddish discoloration on leaf undersides and petioles signals phosphorus deficiency; yellowing between veins while veins remain green is a classic sign of magnesium or iron deficiency. In all cases, plant growth is stunted and fruit yield and quality decline regardless of which specific nutrient is limiting.",
            causes = "Primary drivers include poor or depleted soil, incorrect soil pH (chili can no longer efficiently absorb nutrients outside the 6.0–6.8 range regardless of soil levels), and waterlogged or compacted roots that physically cannot take up nutrients. Excessive watering that continually leaches nutrients from the root zone is another common cause.",
            treatment = "Check drainage and soil pH first, as waterlogged roots or improper pH block nutrient uptake. Add well-rotted compost and feed gently with a balanced vegetable fertilizer following package directions, then watch new growth for improvement. Consult a local agricultural extension office for a soil test if symptoms persist.",
            preventionTips = "Test soil pH before planting, enrich garden beds with compost, avoid overwatering that leaches nutrients from the root zone, and maintain a regular balanced feeding routine following fertilizer label directions.",
            careTips = "Leaf yellowing or discoloration often points to nutrient stress, though similar symptoms can also result from waterlogged roots, poor drainage, or improper soil pH, so confirm growing conditions or test soil pH before heavy fertilizing. Ensure the soil drains freely and is not compacted or overwatered. Work well-rotted compost into the topsoil around the root zone and apply a balanced vegetable fertilizer gently according to package directions, starting conservatively on stressed plants. Monitor new growth rather than older leaves, as newly emerging foliage is where improvement appears first. If discoloration persists, consult a local agricultural extension office for a soil or tissue test to confirm the exact nutrient needed."
        ),

        "Chili_White_Spot" to DiseaseResult(
            diseaseName = "White Spot / Powdery Mildew (Chili)",
            description = "White powdery or mealy-looking patches spread across the upper surface of leaves, occasionally extending to undersides and stems. Unlike most fungal diseases, it appears on dry, intact leaf surfaces rather than on wet or damaged tissue. Heavily affected leaves curl, turn yellow, and drop early. If the white marks are raised, rough, or silvery rather than powdery, the cause may be thrips feeding rather than mildew.",
            causes = "Powdery appearance is almost always Leveillula taurica, a fungus uniquely capable of infecting leaves without free water on the surface — it spreads fastest in warm days (25–30°C) combined with high relative humidity and stagnant air inside dense canopies. Raised, silvery-white scarring is typically thrips (Thrips tabaci or Frankliniella spp.) feeding damage, which leaves a physically different, rough texture compared to the wipe-able powder of true mildew.",
            treatment = "Confirm whether patches wipe off like powder (powdery mildew) or feel rough and silvery (thrips feeding)—check with a local garden advisor if uncertain. Prune heavily affected leaves into trash and improve airflow. For mildew, use a labeled garden sulfur or potassium bicarbonate spray; for thrips, apply insecticidal soap or a labeled garden control to leaf undersides per label directions.",
            preventionTips = "Provide generous spacing and airflow around plants, avoid excess nitrogen fertilizer that stimulates overly tender growth, keep plants steadily watered at the soil base, and use sticky traps to watch for thrips.",
            careTips = "Inspect the white patches closely before treating, as visual symptoms can overlap between powdery mildew (a soft white powder that wipes off) and thrips feeding damage (rough, silvery scarring)—confirm with a local garden center or agricultural specialist if uncertain. Prune and discard the most heavily affected leaves in sealed trash, avoid excess nitrogen fertilizer that produces overly tender growth, and thin dense foliage to improve air circulation. For confirmed powdery mildew, a labeled home-garden sulfur or potassium bicarbonate spray applied according to product directions can help manage spread while avoiding hot, direct midday sun. For confirmed thrips, target leaf undersides and growing tips with insecticidal soap or another labeled garden option, and monitor nearby plants regularly."
        ),

        // ==========================================
        // EGGPLANT DISEASES
        // ==========================================
        "Eggplant_Cercospora Leaf Spot" to DiseaseResult(
            diseaseName = "Cercospora Leaf Spot (Eggplant)",
            description = "Round to irregular spots with gray or pale papery centers and darker brown-to-purple borders develop on eggplant leaves, typically starting on older foliage near the plant base. Spots can merge into large dead patches, and heavy infections cause premature leaf drop that exposes developing fruit directly to intense sun, causing sunscald and reducing marketable yield significantly.",
            causes = "Caused by Cercospora fungus, which thrives in warm, humid conditions (25–30°C) and extended periods of leaf wetness. Spores travel by wind and water splash, and they survive in fallen crop debris in the soil between growing seasons — making field sanitation and crop rotation critical management tools alongside fungicide.",
            treatment = "Remove spotted lower leaves into sealed trash, add clean mulch around the base to reduce soil splash, and water strictly at soil level. If lesions continue spreading, consult a local garden advisor about a labeled garden fungicide for eggplants and follow product directions.",
            preventionTips = "Use soil-level or drip watering rather than overhead watering, space plants widely for airflow, clear crop debris into household trash after harvest rather than composting, and rotate eggplants to a fresh bed in future seasons.",
            careTips = "Remove and bag spotted lower leaves and dispose of them in regular trash rather than composting, cleaning pruning tools afterward. Apply a layer of clean mulch around the base of the plant to reduce soil splash onto lower foliage, and water exclusively at the soil line so leaves stay dry. Ensure good spacing and drainage around the roots so the plant stays vigorous. If fungal lesions continue advancing up the plant, consult a local garden advisor about an appropriate labeled garden fungicide for eggplants and follow all label instructions. Rotate eggplants to a different growing area in future seasons and clear crop debris after harvest."
        ),

        "Eggplant_Healthy Leaf" to DiseaseResult(
            diseaseName = "Healthy Eggplant",
            description = "Foliage is deep, uniform green, firm, and free of spots, holes, curling, or discoloration. Stems are upright and sturdy. Growth is steady with normal flower production and fruit set, and fruit develops without deformity, cracking, or blemishes.",
            causes = "The result of consistently good agricultural management: proper plant spacing (45–60 cm minimum) for airflow, correct irrigation depth and timing, balanced fertilization, adequate sunlight (6+ hours daily), and regular scouting that identifies pest and disease pressure early before establishment.",
            treatment = "No active disease is present. Continue deep soil-level watering, clean mulching, balanced feeding per package instructions, and regular leaf inspections.",
            preventionTips = "Keep generous spacing between plants for airflow, maintain mulch around the base, prune lower leaves touching the soil, and inspect both leaf surfaces regularly for flea beetles, aphids, or early spots.",
            careTips = "Your eggplant looks healthy with no signs of active disease. Keep watering deeply at soil level to keep leaves dry, maintain clean mulch around the base to conserve moisture and block soil splash, and ensure adequate plant spacing with full sunlight. Feed regularly with a balanced vegetable fertilizer following package directions, prune any lower leaves touching the soil, and inspect both leaf surfaces routinely for flea beetles, aphids, or early spots."
        ),

        "Eggplant_Insect Pest Disease" to DiseaseResult(
            diseaseName = "Insect Pest Infestation (Eggplant)",
            description = "Visible feeding damage appears as small round shot-holes across leaf surfaces (flea beetles), large ragged sections of stripped foliage (hornworms), or curled and sticky new growth covered with soft-bodied insects (aphids). Honeydew secreted by aphids encourages secondary sooty mold growth. Heavy infestations can defoliate a plant rapidly and, in the case of aphids, may also transmit plant viruses.",
            causes = "Flea beetles (Epitrix spp.) chew characteristic small round shot-holes and are worst in warm, dry weather on young transplants. Aphids cluster on tender new growth and reproduce extremely fast in mild temperatures — populations can explode within days. Hornworms (Manduca spp.) are large green caterpillars that feed heavily at night and can strip large sections of foliage within 48 hours if not caught early.",
            treatment = "Identify the exact pest first—check with a local garden advisor if unsure. Hand-pick large caterpillars, crush visible eggs on leaf undersides, rinse light aphids off with water, and when needed use targeted home-garden options like insecticidal soap, neem oil, or a caterpillar-specific biological product per label instructions.",
            preventionTips = "Use floating row covers on young transplants before flowering, keep surrounding beds free of weeds, preserve beneficial insects like ladybugs and lacewings, and inspect leaf undersides routinely.",
            careTips = "Inspect the plant closely to identify the specific pest before taking action, as management varies between flea beetles (tiny round shot-holes), aphids (clusters of soft-bodied insects on tender shoots), and caterpillars such as hornworms (large ragged leaf damage)—verify with a local garden advisor if unsure. Hand-pick large caterpillars and crush visible eggs on leaf undersides, or rinse light aphid clusters off with a steady spray of water. Keep the surrounding area free of weeds where pests shelter, and consider floating row covers on young plants before flowering. When needed, apply an appropriate home-garden treatment such as insecticidal soap, neem oil, or a caterpillar-specific biological product strictly according to label directions while avoiding broad-spectrum sprays that harm beneficial insects like ladybugs."
        ),

        "Eggplant_Leaf Spot Disease" to DiseaseResult(
            diseaseName = "General Leaf Spot (Eggplant)",
            description = "Irregular brown or black spots form across the leaf surface. Fungal spots typically have a dry, papery texture with a yellow halo (Alternaria or Phomopsis); bacterial spots appear water-soaked, greasy, and translucent, often without a distinct raised margin. Both types cause yellowing and early leaf drop, weakening the plant's ability to support fruit through photosynthesis.",
            causes = "Fungal causes include Alternaria solani and Phomopsis vexans, both favored by warm humid conditions and splashing water. Water-soaked, greasy-looking lesions instead point to bacterial pathogens (Pseudomonas or Xanthomonas species) common in prolonged wet weather. Distinguishing between fungal and bacterial is critical because the treatments are different.",
            treatment = "Prune and discard spotted leaves in sealed trash, sanitize shears with rubbing alcohol between cuts, and water strictly at soil level. Because fungal and bacterial leaf spots need different products, confirm the cause with a local garden advisor before applying a labeled fungicide or copper bactericide.",
            preventionTips = "Space plants generously for airflow, ensure soil drains well, keep foliage dry by watering at the base, and rotate eggplants out of the same bed in future seasons.",
            careTips = "Prune and discard infected leaves in sealed trash rather than composting, and disinfect shears with rubbing alcohol between cuts. Water strictly at the base of the plant to keep foliage dry, improve drainage if water pools around the roots, and ensure plenty of sunlight and airflow so leaves dry quickly. Because general leaf spots on eggplant can be caused by either fungal or bacterial pathogens that require different products, have persistent or rapidly spreading symptoms confirmed by a local agricultural extension officer or garden center before applying a targeted garden fungicide or copper bactericide according to label directions."
        ),

        "Eggplant_Mosaic Virus Disease" to DiseaseResult(
            diseaseName = "Mosaic Virus (Eggplant)",
            description = "Leaves develop a mottled pattern of alternating light and dark green patches, often with distorted, wrinkled, or curled leaf margins. New growth is severely stunted, and internodes shorten. Fruit that does form is typically small, misshapen, and discolored. The plant rarely dies outright but essentially stops being productive.",
            causes = "Caused by multiple potyviruses (Potato Virus Y, Tobacco Mosaic Virus, Cucumber Mosaic Virus) spread primarily by aphids during sap-feeding. Critically, insecticides do not effectively prevent transmission of these viruses — aphids inject virus into plants within seconds of landing, before insecticide residue can kill them. Reflective silver mulch is more effective than insecticides at preventing aphid-borne virus transmission because it repels aphids before they land. The virus also spreads via contaminated tools and hands.",
            treatment = "No product cures a virus-infected plant. Uproot and seal affected plants in household trash immediately—never compost them—wash hands with soap, sanitize tools, and manage aphids on healthy surrounding plants with insecticidal soap or neem oil per label directions.",
            preventionTips = "Consider reflective mulch before transplanting to deter aphids, clear weeds around the bed, sanitize tools between plants, and remove symptomatic plants promptly to protect healthy crops.",
            careTips = "There is no cure for plant mosaic viruses. To protect surrounding plants, carefully uproot and seal the infected eggplant in a trash bag for disposal—never compost virus-infected material. Wash your hands thoroughly with soap and water and sanitize all pruning shears, stakes, and ties that touched the plant using diluted household bleach or rubbing alcohol before handling other plants. Clear weeds around the planting area and manage aphids on nearby healthy plants using insecticidal soap or neem oil per label instructions, or consider reflective mulch in future plantings to deter aphids."
        ),

        "Eggplant_Powdery Mildew" to DiseaseResult(
            diseaseName = "Powdery Mildew (Eggplant)",
            description = "White, powdery fungal patches spread across the upper surfaces of leaves, sometimes covering entire leaves in severe cases. Affected leaves yellow, curl upward, and drop early. Unlike most fungal diseases, powdery mildew does not require free water on leaf surfaces to infect — it spreads in warm days with high humidity and can be widespread even during dry weather if airflow around the canopy is poor.",
            causes = "Caused primarily by Leveillula taurica in eggplant, a fungal pathogen unique in that it does not need wet leaves to infect or spread — only high relative humidity and warm temperatures (20–30°C) combined with poor air circulation. Overcrowded plantings and excessive nitrogen fertilization (which produces soft, fast-growing tissue) significantly increase susceptibility.",
            treatment = "Thin crowded foliage to increase airflow, prune heavily coated leaves into sealed trash, and water steadily at the base. For active mildew, consider a labeled garden potassium bicarbonate, neem oil, or sulfur product applied carefully per label directions while avoiding hot direct sun.",
            preventionTips = "Space plants generously for airflow, increase ventilation in enclosed growing areas, avoid excessive nitrogen fertilizer, and keep plants steadily hydrated at the root zone.",
            careTips = "Improve airflow through the plant by gently thinning crowded foliage and keeping plants well spaced; if growing in an enclosed space, increase ventilation to lower humidity. Prune heavily coated leaves and dispose of them in regular trash to lower spore levels, avoid excessive nitrogen fertilizing, and water steadily at the soil base to prevent drought stress. For active mildew, consider a home-garden potassium bicarbonate, neem oil, or sulfur product applied carefully according to label directions—avoid applying sulfur or oils in intense direct sun or high heat to prevent leaf scorch."
        ),

        // ==========================================
        // TOMATO DISEASES
        // ==========================================
        "Tomato_Bacterial_spot" to DiseaseResult(
            diseaseName = "Bacterial Spot (Tomato)",
            description = "Small, dark, greasy-looking spots appear on leaves, stems, and fruit — often with a yellow halo on leaves and raised, scab-like lesions on green fruit that reduce marketability. Heavy leaf infection causes defoliation and exposes fruit to sunscald. Spots are most commonly seen first on older, lower leaves before progressing upward.",
            causes = "Caused by Xanthomonas perforans (the dominant species on tomato) spread by splashing water, wind-driven rain, contaminated hands, and tools. Warm, wet weather at 24–30°C drives rapid spread. The disease most commonly enters a new planting through infected seed or infected transplants. Once on tomato fruit, red fruit cannot be infected (high acid content inhibits bacteria), but green fruit can develop scab lesions.",
            treatment = "Prune spotted lower leaves into a trash bag without dropping them onto the soil, sanitize hands and tools, and switch strictly to soil-level watering. If symptoms keep spreading, consult a local garden advisor about a labeled garden copper bactericide and follow product instructions carefully.",
            preventionTips = "Plant certified disease-free seed and healthy transplants, avoid working on wet vines, sanitize stakes and cages between seasons, clear fallen debris, and rotate tomatoes out of the same bed.",
            careTips = "Prune spotted lower leaves carefully into a bag without dropping them onto the soil, and disinfect hands and shears with rubbing alcohol before touching healthy plants. Switch completely to soil-level watering so foliage stays dry, and avoid handling tomato vines when wet. Avoid heavy high-nitrogen feeding that stimulates overly lush, susceptible foliage, and clear fallen leaf debris from the bed. If bacterial spot continues spreading, consult a local garden center or agricultural extension advisor about a labeled garden copper bactericide and apply strictly per label directions. Prevent future outbreaks by planting disease-free seeds or transplants, sanitizing stakes and cages, and rotating crops."
        ),

        "Tomato_Early_blight" to DiseaseResult(
            diseaseName = "Early Blight (Tomato)",
            description = "Dark brown lesions with a distinctive concentric ring 'bullseye' or 'target' pattern appear first on the oldest lower leaves, then progress upward as the season advances. Leaves yellow around lesions and drop, leaving the lower plant bare. In advanced cases, stem lesions (dark, sunken, and collar-like near soil level) and fruit lesions (sunken, dark, leathery) can also develop.",
            causes = "Caused by Alternaria linariae (formerly A. solani) fungus, which overwinters in infected soil debris and on volunteer tomato plants, then splashes up onto lower leaves during rain or irrigation. The fungus requires alternating wet and dry periods at warm temperatures (24–29°C) for rapid spread. Spores spread primarily by water splash and air currents. Plants under nitrogen stress are significantly more susceptible.",
            treatment = "Prune lower leaves close to the soil into sealed trash, apply clean mulch around the base to stop spore splash, and water strictly at root level. If spots continue advancing upward, consider a labeled garden fungicide for tomatoes following package directions.",
            preventionTips = "Keep the lower canopy trimmed above the soil, mulch around plant bases, stake or trellis vines for strong airflow, maintain steady balanced nutrition, and rotate tomato beds across seasons.",
            careTips = "Prune lower leaves touching or close to the soil surface and dispose of them in sealed trash—do not compost infected foliage. Add a clean layer of straw or garden mulch around the base to block soil-borne spores from splashing onto leaves, stake or trellis plants for strong airflow, and water strictly at the root zone. Keep plants steadily nourished with a balanced tomato fertilizer per package directions, as stressed plants are more vulnerable. If target-like spots continue moving upward, consider a labeled garden fungicide for tomatoes following product instructions, and practice crop rotation in future seasons."
        ),

        "Tomato_Late_blight" to DiseaseResult(
            diseaseName = "Late Blight (Tomato)",
            description = "Large, dark, water-soaked patches spread rapidly across leaves and stems — sometimes with a pale green-yellow border — and white fuzzy sporulation appears on the underside in humid conditions. Fruit develops firm, brown, greasy-looking rot that renders it completely unmarketable. Late blight can move from the first visible lesion to complete plant destruction within 3–5 days under favorable cool, wet conditions. The same pathogen devastates potatoes.",
            causes = "Caused by Phytophthora infestans, an oomycete (water mold, not a true fungus), which spreads explosively in cool (15–21°C), wet, humid weather. Spores travel by wind up to 30–40 miles from infected fields. The same pathogen affects potato, so proximity to infected potato crops or fields is a significant risk factor. The disease does not overwinter in soil without a living host but arrives on wind or infected transplants each season.",
            treatment = "Inspect nearby tomato and potato plants immediately. Remove isolated early spotted leaves in dry weather, or bag and dispose of heavily infected plants in household trash—never compost them—to protect surrounding crops. Contact a local agricultural extension office promptly for labeled protective fungicide options.",
            preventionTips = "Plant late-blight-resistant tomato varieties when available, avoid overhead or evening watering, keep vines well spaced and ventilated, do not work on wet foliage, and avoid planting right beside potatoes.",
            careTips = "Late blight spreads very rapidly in cool, wet, humid weather and can affect both tomatoes and potatoes. Inspect all nearby tomato and potato plants immediately. If only an isolated leaf shows an early spot, remove and bag it immediately during dry weather; however, if multiple leaves or stems are already affected, pull and seal the entire plant in a trash bag for disposal—never compost it—to protect neighboring plants. Stop all overhead watering, avoid evening watering, and improve airflow around remaining plants. Consult a local agricultural extension office promptly regarding protective garden fungicides labeled for late blight, and consider late-blight-resistant tomato varieties in future plantings."
        ),

        "Tomato_Leaf_Mold" to DiseaseResult(
            diseaseName = "Leaf Mold (Tomato)",
            description = "Pale green to yellow spots develop on the upper leaf surface, with a corresponding patch of olive-green to gray-purple fuzzy sporulation on the underside directly below. Affected leaves curl, dry out, and drop. In enclosed growing spaces (greenhouses, tunnels, high-humidity plots), the disease can spread rapidly from a few spots to complete defoliation of the lower canopy within weeks.",
            causes = "Caused by Passalora fulva (formerly Fulvia fulva) fungus, which requires sustained relative humidity above 85% to sporulate and spread. It is primarily a disease of enclosed or sheltered growing environments — open-field cases are rare unless in naturally humid microclimates. The fungus does not produce spores at low humidity even if the plants are already infected.",
            treatment = "Maximize ventilation and lower humidity first—open vents, run fans in enclosed shelters, prune crowded foliage, and water at soil level in the early morning. Remove leaves with fuzzy underside mold into sealed trash, and consult a garden advisor about a labeled fungicide if humid conditions persist.",
            preventionTips = "Prune lower leaves and dense side-shoots to open the canopy, space plants widely in humid environments, keep greenhouse air circulating, and select resistant tomato varieties when available.",
            careTips = "Reducing humidity and maximizing air circulation are the most important steps for managing tomato leaf mold. Prune crowded lower leaves and side-shoots to open the canopy, increase spacing where possible, and keep vents open or use airflow fans if growing in an enclosed shelter or greenhouse. Water only at the soil base in the early morning so foliage dries well before evening, and remove leaves showing fuzzy olive-gray mold on the undersides into sealed trash. If humid conditions persist and spotting spreads, consult a local garden advisor about an appropriate labeled garden fungicide, ensuring thorough coverage of leaf undersides per label directions."
        ),

        "Tomato_Septoria_leaf_spot" to DiseaseResult(
            diseaseName = "Septoria Leaf Spot (Tomato)",
            description = "Numerous small (3–6 mm), circular spots with dark brown borders, gray-to-tan centers, and tiny black specks (pycnidia — fungal fruiting bodies) visible at the center appear first on the oldest lower leaves. Heavy infection causes rapid yellowing and complete defoliation of the lower plant, moving upward progressively. Unlike early blight, Septoria rarely attacks fruit but the defoliation it causes weakens the plant severely over the season.",
            causes = "Caused by Septoria lycopersici fungus, which overwinters in infected crop debris and volunteer solanaceous plants in the soil. Splash dispersal during rain or irrigation moves spores from soil debris onto lower leaves. Wet foliage for extended periods at 20–25°C is optimal for infection. Spores spread readily by contact — on hands, tools, and clothing — making sanitation critical.",
            treatment = "Remove spotted lower leaves carefully into a trash bag without shaking them, mulch around the base to block soil splash, and water strictly at soil level. If spotting continues moving upward, apply an appropriate labeled garden fungicide for tomatoes following package directions.",
            preventionTips = "Mulch plant bases cleanly from planting onward, never handle wet foliage, wash hands and sanitize tools after working on plants, clear debris into trash after harvest, and rotate tomato beds.",
            careTips = "Carefully remove spotted lower leaves into a trash bag without shaking them, and never compost infected foliage. Lay clean mulch around the base of the plant to block soil-borne spores from splashing onto healthy leaves, and water only at soil level. Avoid working among tomato plants while leaves are wet, and wash hands and disinfect tools after handling affected foliage. If spotting continues advancing upward, apply an appropriate labeled garden fungicide for tomatoes according to package instructions, and rotate tomatoes to a different bed in future seasons."
        ),

        "Tomato_Spider_mites_Two_spotted_spider_mite" to DiseaseResult(
            diseaseName = "Spider Mites (Tomato)",
            description = "Fine yellow stippling (tiny white or yellow dots) appears across the leaf surface as mites pierce individual cells to feed. Fine silky webbing forms on leaf undersides and between leaf axils and stems as infestation increases. Leaves turn bronze or papery and may dry out completely as feeding damage accumulates. Spider mite populations can double within 3–5 days in hot, dry conditions, making early action critical.",
            causes = "Caused by the two-spotted spider mite (Tetranychus urticae), an arachnid pest (not an insect) that thrives in hot (above 27°C), dry, dusty conditions. Plants under drought stress are significantly more attractive to mites and suffer faster damage. Broad-spectrum insecticide overuse kills natural mite predators (predatory mites, ladybugs), causing population flare-ups. Webbing on the leaf underside signals a heavy infestation — mites protected under webbing are significantly harder to reach with miticides.",
